  <h2>The biggest difference: how rent is paid</h2>

  <p>
    In Dubai, rent is almost never paid monthly by direct debit. Instead, it's paid upfront
    by <strong>post-dated cheques</strong> — cheques that you write at the start of the
    tenancy, dated throughout the year, which the landlord deposits on each due date.
  </p>

  <p>
    The most common arrangements are:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>1 cheque</strong> — full annual rent paid upfront (gives you the best negotiating position and often a lower price)</li>
    <li><strong>2 cheques</strong> — split across 6 months</li>
    <li><strong>4 cheques</strong> — quarterly payments</li>
    <li><strong>12 cheques</strong> — monthly, increasingly available but landlords typically charge more for this flexibility</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout--tip">
    <span class="callout__heading">✦ Practical tip</span>
    <p>
      You'll need a UAE bank account to issue cheques. This is a genuine chicken-and-egg
      situation — you need a visa for a bank account, and ideally a bank account before
      you sign a tenancy. Many new arrivals stay in a hotel apartment or short-term rental
      for their first few weeks while they sort the paperwork. See our
      <a href="/dubai/banking">Dubai Banking guide</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What it costs</h2>

  <p>
    Dubai rents vary significantly by area, building quality and property type. As a general
    guide for a one-bedroom apartment (prices change — always check current listings):
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dubai Marina / JBR</strong> — premium waterfront, higher prices</li>
    <li><strong>Downtown / Business Bay</strong> — central, prestigious, range of prices</li>
    <li><strong>JLT (Jumeirah Lakes Towers)</strong> — more affordable than Marina, good community</li>
    <li><strong>Deira / Bur Dubai</strong> — most affordable, more traditional areas</li>
    <li><strong>JVC (Jumeirah Village Circle)</strong> — affordable, newer developments</li>
    <li><strong>Mirdif / Rashidiya</strong> — popular with families, more space for the price</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Check Property Finder or Bayut for current rental prices in specific areas — the market
    moves and any figures we publish will quickly become outdated.
  </p>

  <h2>Upfront costs</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Security deposit</strong> — typically 5% of annual rent for unfurnished properties, 10% for furnished</li>
    <li><strong>Agency fee</strong> — usually 5% of annual rent, paid to the real estate agent</li>
    <li><strong>Ejari registration fee</strong> — a small government fee to register your tenancy contract (mandatory)</li>
    <li><strong>DEWA connection</strong> — Dubai Electricity and Water Authority, you must open an account when you move in. There's a refundable deposit (AED 2,000 for apartments, AED 4,000 for villas) plus connection fees.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Ejari — registering your tenancy</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Ejari</strong> (Arabic for "my rent") is the Dubai Land Department's mandatory
    system for registering tenancy contracts. Your landlord or agent registers the contract
    on the Ejari system, and you receive a certificate confirming you're a legal tenant.
    This is important: without Ejari, you can't get a residency visa renewal linked to your
    address, and utilities can be harder to set up.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--tip">
    <span class="callout__heading">✦ Make sure Ejari is done</span>
    <p>
      Always insist on the Ejari registration being completed promptly after signing your
      contract. Keep your Ejari certificate — you'll need it for visa renewals and other
      official processes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Your rights as a tenant under RERA</h2>

  <p>
    The <strong>Real Estate Regulatory Agency (RERA)</strong> governs the rental market in
    Dubai and provides genuine tenant protections:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rent increases are capped</strong> — RERA publishes a Rental Index showing permitted increase percentages based on current market rates. A landlord cannot increase rent by more than the RERA-permitted percentage. Check the current index at <a href="https://dubailand.gov.ae" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dubailand.gov.ae</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>12 months notice to vacate</strong> — if a landlord wants to sell the property or move in themselves, they must give you at least 12 months written notice via a notarised notification.</li>
    <li><strong>Dispute resolution</strong> — the Rental Dispute Centre handles conflicts between landlords and tenants. Filing a case costs a percentage of the annual rent.</li>
    <li><strong>Security deposit return</strong> — must be returned within 30 days of the tenancy ending (minus agreed deductions).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Before you sign: checklist</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>✓ Check the landlord owns the property — request the title deed</li>
    <li>✓ Make sure the agent is RERA-registered (all licensed agents should have a RERA card)</li>
    <li>✓ Check current rent against the RERA Rental Index to ensure it's fair</li>
    <li>✓ Confirm the Ejari will be registered promptly</li>
    <li>✓ Take photos of every room and note any existing damage</li>
    <li>✓ Confirm what's included — maintenance, service charges, chiller (district cooling for AC in some buildings — can be expensive)</li>
    <li>✓ Check whether the building has central AC (chiller) or individual AC units — this affects your DEWA bill significantly</li>
  </ul>

